A||||16||Richard Parnell||ATF Follow up||||1||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||02-03-2000||07:55 PM||||Just a note re: the ATF controversy.  I still haven"t changed master and slave and flushed out the ATF for DOT3 yet.  I am watching to see what happens over a period of time.  The clutch is getting slower and slower returning to the up position after pushing it in.  The pressure has to be pumped up a few times before each driving session. And most interesting, the black rubber insert that goes under the resevoir cap has swollen to at least 1/3 again it's normal size and is slowly melting.  I removed it and when I did it was like real soft chewing gum stuck to your shoes on a hot day.  Interestink, no?||||reg||1
Z||000001||TOM||02-03-2000||09:01 PM||||The people that I told when this trend started that it was a bad idea. told my that I didnt know what I was talking about and they did it and it worked. Well for a while anyway. The brake and clutch seals are not petroleum compatable.I am an aircraft mechanic I know these things||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick||02-03-2000||11:45 PM||||<BR><B>Tom</B>, this is at least the second time I'm aware of that you have felt compelled to come forward and state you knew that using ATF was a bad idea. Now you're also stating that the people you warned told you that you didn't know what you were talking about. This really puzzles me because I remember nothing of the sort. Out of curiosity, I did a thorough search at this forum. Not only did I not find any criticism by us of this opinion, I also could not find ANY statement made by you regarding the negative effects of using ATF. The original thread that ATF use was suggested in is here:<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000034.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000034.html</A> <P>That thread is 138 posts long. You do not appear in this thread. Humour me Tom, and please show us where you were criticised for speaking up against the use of ATF in the clutch hydraulic system.<P><B>Richard</B>, since it does seem to appear that all the seals are continuing to swell in your clutch hydraulic system, do you not think that it might be worth while to flush the system and reinstall Dot3 before you change you master and slave cylinders? This should stop the swelling and maybe even reverse the process somewhat. It sounds like you may have air in the system anyway, so while you're bleeding it you could change the fluid at that time.||||reg||5
Z||000003||Richard Parnell||02-04-2000||12:06 AM||||Please I didn't mean cause any ill feelings to be generated here.  I only want to pass along information which is what is so great about this forum.  <BR>I do have another question. Before I change my master and slave on the clutch.  <BR>The braided haose leading to the slave, what is on the inside of the braid to make it liquid proof?  Will I need to change this (the braided section) also?||||reg||9
Z||000004||fierospeeder||02-04-2000||12:51 AM||||im not sure about the answer to your question but i would blow the entire line with an airhose if its possible, dont have a stick.  Oil tends to decompose anything rubber.  I had an oil leak and it would get on the engine mount.  After i redone my oilpan, i looked at the engine mount and it was all broken down and the bolts fell out.  The belt thats on the pulleys also break down when oil is in contact.  A friend told me you have to use a trans line when replacing the hose i have leaking because ordinary vacuum or fuel hose will decompose with trans fluid and clog up the insides of a trans.||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||02-04-2000||02:04 AM||||After looking back at the thread in question, I too had doubts about the use of ATF. The use of Mobil 1 Oil (suggested by David) was alarming enough. Ogre suggested ATF if you were going to use something other than DOT 3 because it would be more friendly to plastic. He also mentioned that he used ATF in floor jacks. I don't recall ever seeing anybody really saying that they had used ATF before. People sure did jump on it though. My reaction was to wait and see. I have seen a lot of things that cars shouldn't do that blatanly defy mechanical logic. Who was I to say that it wouldn't work.<P>But I sure wasn't going to be the guinea pig. <P>So what Tom was saying was what I was thinking at the time. This ATF thing seemed to pop up through many different threads at one time or another.  Its hard to say I told you so at someone else's suffering. So I won't because I didn't. But its hard not to say I thought so. I am sorry Richard that you are having to go through this. I would say we all learned from it. <p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 02-04-2000).]||||reg||9
Z||000006||mwbackus||02-04-2000||02:18 AM||||When I objected to the use of ATF back in Dec. 99, I did not feel my criticism to be unwelcome:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001667.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001667.html</A>  <P>This forum has been much more open to constructive ideas than any other Fiero resource I have used.  One can only hope that an objective evaluation of these ideas will be considered a welcome and valuable asset to all those involved.<P>  <BR>Mike<BR><p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 02-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Patrick||02-04-2000||03:12 AM||||<BR>I admit that I was one of the first ones to jump on the ATF bandwagon. I didn't mind being a "guinea pig" because I had nothing to lose. I also admit that I did my part in convincing others to try it, but only because I was anxious to help others out the same way that I was helped. Yes, there were some reservations expressed by some forum members. I distinctly remember the comments made by Mike. I do not remember any made by Tom. It's not the fact that Tom may or may not have said anything at the time that's bothering me. What it is that's got me choked is the accusation that members of this forum, who were promoting the use of ATF, criticised anyone else for suggesting that maybe it wasn't a good idea. I feel bad enough as it is that several forum members have had problems with the use of ATF without having to read that one of us gave Tom a bad time about something he said. Especially if we can't find any evidence that Tom ever said anything about ATF in the first place. <P>For whatever reason, my clutch with ATF continues to work fine.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||02-04-2000||11:25 AM||||Yes, I use ATF in jacks. always works better than jack oil when it's cold. One of my jacks has had ATF in it for at least a decade.<P>As I said before,,,, I wish I had remembered a particular master cylinder when we started this. I tried to store it by using oil to prevent rust..... I prevented the rust, but wrecked the seals. (It was over 20 years ago now.......)<P>Some people may be having better luck because some seals may be slightly diferant in the formulation of the materials used to make them. Or the tolerances in their cylinders are more loose. Or both.<P>There has to be something besides DOT3 that would give better lubrication and better corosion resistance, but won't eat the seals.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phil||02-04-2000||11:57 AM||||Undiluted antifreeze?||||reg||1
Z||000010||WKDFIRO||02-04-2000||01:38 PM||||What is the deficiency with using DOT 3? I live in So Cal so I would not know the problems associated with places in the country with seasons. Heck, it hit 90 degrees on Wednesday in Orange County! Isn't it February?||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||02-04-2000||01:38 PM||||Mr Parnell,<BR>If you see yourself making the switch to back to DOT3, and need to replace the seals, I recommend pulling them now, while they still slide fairly easily. I ended up buying new master and slaves. I think it took 120psi of compressed air to blow out the seals in the master. For the slave, I took the compressor up to 145psi, those seals are still stuck in the cylinder. The braided steel hose: I just left mine be, if it isn't leaking now, I don't think you'll have any problems to it from the ATF. Possibly because the rubber is older in the line.<BR>I leave you to decide what to use, but I'm not using ATF again. This guinea pig is retired from testing.<P>Phil: I'll let you try that. What color you have in mind?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Phil||02-04-2000||06:55 PM||||I figure pink to match my eyes.||||reg||1
Z||000013||lowCG||02-04-2000||08:00 PM||||If a bending pedal is a problem;fix it,if a side load on the slave cylinder is a problem;fix it.<BR>I have never seen any great mystery with a Fiero clutch,frustration yes,but once all the air was gone,no problems.<BR>I've used hydraulic jack oil in Koni shocks and struts for a few years,not much rubber in those though.<BR>Hydraulic systems have the tightest tolerances on the whole car,||||reg||1
Z||000014||Eric||02-04-2000||10:13 PM||||Remember there are two braided hoses on the clutch line, one in back which you can see and one up front which you can't see.<P>What about DOT 5 fluid?  I've also heard of a plasic friendly ATF, but can't seem to remember who makes it.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Phaeton||02-05-2000||03:50 AM||||I tried cleaning my contact lens with isopropyl alcohol and they swoll up bigger than a quarter.  I guess the moral is either isopropyl won't work in the clutch or I need more dialkyldithiophosphate in my engine.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Ozzy||02-06-2000||11:44 PM||||Hi Folks...<P>I can say at this point, after 7 months post ATF transfusion, that this is probably the ONLY part of the car that has not given me any trouble. <P>Sorry for the folks who are having trouble. Are some of the seals from different kit manufacturors made of different materials??<BR>Just a thought.<P>Ozzy<p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 02-06-2000).]||||reg||1
